Only repaint the whole page for a root/body style change if the BACKGROUND changed.

Supersedes webkit-root-customprop-repaint-skip-env.patch (which only handled the
custom-property case). RenderBox::styleWillChange takes any Repaint-level style
change on <html>/<body> and calls view().repaintRootContents() - repainting the
ENTIRE root layer (29883-93000px tall on franceinfo). Per WebKit's own comment
there, this exists ONLY because the root/body BACKGROUND propagates up to the
canvas. Every other style change on the root - a class toggle (nav/menu/scrolled
state), a CSS custom property (franceinfo's --offset-sticky-* written every scroll
frame) - does not paint the canvas; the elements whose rendering actually changed
are repainted through the normal renderer-level repaint path.

Device-proven with ATLANTIC_REPAINT_BT symbolized backtraces:
- during scroll: the custom-property writes fire this every frame,
- while merely SETTLING (no scroll): class toggles fire it 6x on one load
  (rect 1080x29883 of 1080x29883) - the user-visible "icons blinking several times
  before settling down",
and each one dirties ~the whole viewport's tiles (~2280 per scroll update), which
lock-steps the compositor (isWaitingForTiles) into a multi-second freeze.

WEBKIT_SKIP_ROOT_REPAINT_UNLESS_BACKGROUND (default ON, =0 restores stock): take the
full-page repaint only when the background actually changed - its color
(visitedDependentBackgroundColor, what propagates to the canvas), its presence
(hasBackground), or its layers (backgroundLayers: images/position/attachment).
The rootLayerConfigurationChanged() fixed-background check is preserved inside.

--- a/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderBox.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderBox.cpp
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #include "config.h"
 #include "RenderBox.h"
 
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include "AnchorPositionEvaluator.h"
 #include "BackgroundPainter.h"
 #include "BorderPainter.h"
@@ -264,9 +265,36 @@
